# Research activities on perovskite solar cells in China

Yanbo Wang & Liyuan Han
State Key Laboratory of Metal Matrix Composites, Shanghai Jiao Tong University, Shanghai 200240, China eceived January 23, 2019; accepted March 7, 2019; published online April 2, 2019
Perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have attracted much attention because of their high efficiencies and low costs for production. Although academic research started late in China, compared to that in Europe and Korea, the majority of active PSC research is now conducted in China; furthermore, Chinese research groups currently hold the certified highest efficiency record for both an individual PSC and a PSC module. China is also the world’s largest supplier of solar modules, making it a promising country in which to realize the commercialization of PSCs. Herein, we review PSC research activities undertaken in China (both academic and industrial) and discuss significant remaining challenges to overcome for early commercialization of PSCs. We propose that research activities shift away from material and device structure development toward improving PSC stability and developing methods for large-area module fabrication. In addition, we suggest that a recognized certification center is urgently needed in China to further accelerate PSC research.
## perovskite solar cell, high efficiency, stability, commercialization, China
Citation: Wang Y, Han L. Research activities on perovskite solar cells in China. Sc China Chem, 2019, 62, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11426-019-9461-1
## 1 Introduction
The first use of an organic-inorganic metal halide perovskite material in a solar cell was reported by Miyasaka et al. (Japan) in 2009 [1]. In 2012, Snaith et al. (UK) [2] reported the first all-solid-state stable perovskite solar cell (PSC); nearly simultaneously, Park et al. (Korea) [3] reported a similar PSC. Since then, the efficiency of PSC has increased rapidly with the development of improved methods for the production of high-quality perovskite crystals (Figure 1(a)) [4–7]. During the pioneering stages, PSC research was concentrated in Japan; but state-of-the-art PSC research soon moved to Europe and Korea. By 2017, almost all of the world’s PSC efficiency records were held by the Korea Research Institute of Chemical Technology.
Thus, PSC research in China started relatively late; however, development in this field has been rapid. In 2018, Chinese researchers twice broke the efficiency record for a
PSC [8]. The number of research papers published by Chinese research groups has also rapidly increased yearly, with more than $40\%$ of the world’s PSC research papers now being published by Chinese research groups (Figure 1(b)). There is no doubt that China is now at the forefront of academic research for PSCs.
In this review, the PSC research activities being undertaken in China, in both academia and industry, are presented and the most urgent issues that need to be addressed for the early commercialization of PSCs in China are discussed.
## 2 PSC research in academia
High power conversion efficiency and stability, and low production cost are essential for the practical use of PSCs. Power conversion efficiency and stability are the most important factors because these have a marked effect on the overall cost of a PSC. Therefore, many research groups are actively engaged in improving the efficiency and stability of PSCs.
## 2.1 Power conversion efficiency
The origins of PSCs are rooted in dye-sensitized solar cells, and many current PSC researchers started as dye-sensitized solar cell researchers. Thus, research for normal-structured PSCs (i.e., electrode/electron transport material/perovskite/ hole transport material/electrode, Figure 2(a, b)) started earlier than that for inverted planar PSCs (i.e., electrode/hole transport material/perovskite/electron transport material/ electrode, Figure 2(c)) [10]. In 2013, a normal planar structure without the mesoporous layer was developed and is now a viable alternative PSC structure (Figure 2(b)).
## 2.1.1 Normal-structured PSCs
The traditional electron-transporting material in normalstructured PSCs is $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . The development of PSCs has been greatly advanced through continuous optimization of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer. Liu et al. [11] used a room-temperature method (magnetron sputtering), instead of the conventional hightemperature method, to fabricate a very dense $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer that may be useful for developing a high-performance flexible PSC. Fang et al. [12] prepared a compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ film by thermal oxidation of a sputtered titanium film that suppressed recombination at the interface between $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and perovskite. Our group [13] has investigated the effect of nanoscale pinholes in compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layers and reported that $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layers fabricated by atomic layer deposition had a much lower pinhole density than $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layers fabricated by spin coating or spray pyrolysis.
Some research groups have examined surface modifications and doping of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer to improve its electron mobility and band alignment with the absorbing layer. The ionic liquid 1-butyl-3-methylimidazolium tetrafluoroborate has been used to optimize the surface of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer [14]; however, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is sensitive to ultraviolet light [15]. As an alternative to $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , Fang et al. [16] prepared a planar PSC using $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ as the electron transport material and achieved an average efficiency of $16\%$ (Figure 3).
On the basis of this previous work, You et al. [17] took advantage of the passivation effect of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ on a perovskite film (Figure 4) to produce a PSC with an efficiency of $20\%$ , making $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ a viable electron-transporting material for use in normal-structured PSCs. Furthermore, by optimizing the amount of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ used, they produced a PSC with a certified efficiency approaching $21\%$ [18]. Subsequently, Liu et al. [19] modified $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ with ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid to triple the electron mobility of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ layer and produced a
PSC with an even higher certified efficiency of $21.52\%$ .
In 2018, by applying the knowledge and experience accumulated so far for the fabrication of high-efficiency devices, You group twice broke the world PSC efficiency record, achieving $23.3\%$ and then $23.7\%$ , as reported in Ref. [8]. This achievement exemplifies the efforts that Chinese research groups have made in mastering the core technology used for the preparation of high-efficiency PSCs.
Spiro-MeOTAD is the traditional hole transport material for normal-structured PSCs; but it has low conductivity, which will increase the series resistance and lower the fill factor (FF). Dai et al. [20] demonstrated that P3HT could adsorb firmly on the sidewalls of multi-walled carbon nanotubes (MWNTs), due to $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions, greatly enhancing the FF of solar cells by improving the conductivity of HTM.
## 2.1.2 Inverted PSCs
The inverted PSC was developed by Chen [21]. Compared with the normal structure, the inverted structure has a simpler configuration, longer lifetime, and less hysteresis. By using heavily doped inorganic charge-transporting materials, our group [22] fabricated an inverted PSC with a certified efficiency of $15\%$ for an aperture area of $1{\mathrm{cm}}^{2}$ (Figure 5(a)). This efficiency was comparable to that of other solar cells; thus, PSCs could formally enter the efficiency arena of solar cells [23]. Subsequently, we developed a new PSC structure, the graded heterojunction (Figure 5(b)), that balanced hole and electron diffusion in the perovskite layer. This technology was then used to increase the efficiency record for an inverted PSC to $18.21\%$ [24].
A bottleneck for further improvement of the inverted PSC structure is its low open-circuit voltage compared with the normal PSC structure. Zhu et al. [25] used a solution-processed secondary growth approach to adjust the energy levels of a perovskite film and reduce non-radiative recombination to obtain an open-circuit voltage of $1.21{\mathrm{V}}$ (Figure 6).
## 2.1.3 Others
Perovskite single crystals have been studied by Liu et al. [26]. A perovskite single crystal with a thickness of $120\mathrm{mm}$ was fabricated, sliced into thin wafers, and then used to make a PSC with a device structure of Au/ITO/Spiro-MeOTAD/ perovskite wafer/PCBM/LiF/Ag/Au. The best device showed an open-circuit voltage of $1.15\mathrm{V}$ and a short-circuit current density of $20.02\mathrm{mA/cm}^{2}$ [26]. In addition, the perovskite single crystal demonstrated a much lower concentration of defects and was more suitable than polycrystals for conducting fundamental research. This single perovskite crystal methodology development has contributed much to our understanding of perovskite and the application of perovskite materials [27,28].
Another interesting line of research is the fabrication of perovskite/silicon tandem devices. The tandem structure is expected to provide efficiencies greater than $30\%$ and therefore accelerate the commercialization of PSCs [29]. Recently, Zhang et al. [30] produced a tandem cell with an efficiency of $22.8\%$ .
## 2.2 Stability
Several factors affect the stability of perovskite materials, such as thermal decomposition of methylammonium (MA)- based perovskites or phase transfer of formamidinium-based perovskites at room temperature [31,32]. Although a lot of research has been conducted to increase the efficiency of PSCs, few studies have examined improving PSC stability. When considering PSC stability, the stability of the perovskite film, the charge-transport materials, and the electrodes are all targets for improvement.
## 2.2.1 Stability of the perovskite film
Formamidinium-based perovskites are unstable at room temperature. Our group developed a vertical recrystallization method to stabilize the phase by slow, dropwise, addition methylammonium chloride and achieved a light-soaking and thermal stability up to $500\mathrm{h}$ with an initial efficiency of over $20\%$ [33]. Formamidinium-based perovskites are now the most commonly used perovskite because they have a higher efficiency due to their broader absorption and greater thermal stability than MA-based perovskites.
To examine the possibility of obtaining both high efficiency and thermal stability in a PSC containing a MA-based perovskite, we used two additives to enlarge the grain size and smooth the surface of the perovskite film (Figure 7); this approach greatly reduced the defects in the perovskite crystal, leading to a PSC with a certified efficiency of $19.2\%$ and $80\%$ retention of the initial efficiency after $500\mathrm{{h}}$ of heating at $85~^{\circ}C$ in the dark [34].
Inorganic perovskite materials have attracted increasing attention due to their high thermal stability and potential for use in the fabrication of highly efficient tandem PSC/silicon solar cells. You et al. [35] constructed a high-quality $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{}_{3}$ film via solution-controlled growth, and used this film to fabricate a PSC with a certified efficiency of $14.67\%$ . Furthermore, after exposure to light for $500\mathrm{h}$ , no degradation in efficiency was observed. Zhao et al. [36] gained an even higher efficiency via post-treatment with phenyltrimethylammonium bromide and achieved a steady energy output efficiency of $16.3\%$ .
Fully printable PSCs have been demonstrated to be among the most stable PSCs, not only because a carbon electrode is applied. More importantly, organic salts, such as 5-ammonium valeric acid hydroiodide or 4-(aminomethyl) benzoic acid hydroiodide, were employed to reduce the mismatch between perovskite and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ so as to protect the grain boundaries [37–39].
## 2.2.2 Stability of the charge-transport material
The most commonly used hole-transport materials for normal-structured PSCs are Spiro-MeOTAD and PTAA. Doping the HTM with lithium salts can be used to enhance hole mobility; however, lithium salts absorb water and water is detrimental to the device. To address this issue, we have proposed a dopant-free approach using a highly conductive hole-transport material (TTF-1), which we found improved the stability twofold compared with devices made using Spiro-MeOTAD [40]. Many other research groups, such as the Sun group [41–44], have since reported other dopant-free hole-transport materials, including X44, HA1, HA2, CuPcTIPS, and POZ6-2.
For inverted-structure PSCs, as discussed in Section 2.1.2 (Figure 5(a)), the intrinsic light stability of the inorganic materials in the charge-transport layer were key contributors to these PSCs passing the 1000-h light-soaking stability test [22]. Furthermore, adding graphene to the PCBM (phenylC61-butyric acid methyl ester) layer improved the thermal stability ( $500\mathrm{h}$ thermal stability at $85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ by suppressing ion movement within the cell and preventing oxidation of the $\operatorname{Ag}$
electrode (Figure 8) [45].
## 2.2.3 Stability of the electrode
The most commonly used electrode in PSC is silver, which has been found to form AgI with the extracted iodide ions, which further facilitates the diffusion of iodide, and accelerate the deterioration of the cell [46]. Contrastingly, gold electrodes tend to be more stable, but using gold will increase the fabrication cost of the PSC.
Han et al. [47] proposed a fully printable PSC architecture that used a carbon electrode without a hole conductor (Figure 9). Due to the great stability of a carbon electrode and elimination of the hole-transport layer, the unpackaged device passed a 1000-h light soaking stability test. Furthermore, the stability of this architecture was tested outdoors in a hot desert climate for $^{7\mathrm{d}}$ and indoors under $1056\mathrm{h}$ of continuous light treatment: no drop in efficiency was observed [48].
## 3 PSC development in industry
The first company to focus on developing PSCs was Oxford PV (Oxford, UK). Oxford PV currently holds the world efficiency record for perovskite/silicon tandem cells, close to
$28\%$ , which is greater than the efficiency of mono-crystalline silicon solar cells. Global technology corporations, such as Panasonic and Toshiba in Japan, are also concentrating their efforts to develop next-generation PSC modules.
In China, the Weihua Company (China) was the first to begin fabricating PSC submodules. However, it was the Shanghai Liyuan New Energy Technology Co., Ltd. (China) that was first to produce a PSC module with a record certified efficiency of $12.1\%$ for an aperture area of $36~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ . Subsequently, they developed a PSC module with an increased efficient of $14.17\%$ (Figure 10). Microquanta Semiconductor Co., Ltd. (China) broke its own certified efficiency record for a mini-module three times, with the current record standing at $17.9\%$ for an aperture area of $19.3~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ (Figure 10). WonderSolar Co., Ltd. (China) has concentrated on developing a large-area module using a highly stable carbon electrode. In 2018, they installed a $110\mathrm{m}^{2}$ power system composed from $60\ \mathrm{cm}{\times}60\ \mathrm{cm}$ printable mesoscopic perovskite solar panels fabricated with screen-printing techniques. Other companies, such as GCL New Energy Co., Ltd. (China), are also developing methods of fabricating highquality large-area perovskite films.
The fact that Chinese companies hold the largest share of patents related to PSCs in the world (Figure 11) provides a solid foundation for the future commercialization of PSCs.
## 4 Future prospects
China is gradually dominating the PSC research landscape globally. This is no surprise as China is currently the world’s biggest producer of solar cells. Therefore, it is likely that China will be the first country to successfully commercialize PSCs. Nevertheless, there are still several important issues that must be addressed.
First, more fundamental research into PSCs is needed. Compared with the development of new materials and modification of the device structure, little research has been conducted to elucidate the underlying mechanisms behind PSC operation. For example, PSCs were originally thought to have a p-i-n or n-i-p junction structure. Recently, we reported that the three major cell structures all showed p-n junction behavior, as determined by cross-section kelvin probe force microscopy measurements (Figure 12). Interestingly, we found that the position of the p-n junction in the planar structure changed, depending on the composition of the perovskite crystal [49]. These findings suggest that more research into the underlying mechanisms of PSCs is needed to fully understand the diode properties.
Although highly efficient devices have been developed with predicted costs that are lower than those of traditional energy sources, stability remains a major issue [9,50]. Almost all of the stability data reported to date have been obtained by using the accelerating test originally designed for silicon solar cells (IEC 61215) [51]. However, the accelerating coefficient is different between PSCs and silicon solar cells. Therefore, establishing a stability test standard specifically for PSCs is urgently needed; but, this first requires a better understanding of the decomposition mechanisms of perovskite, charge-transport materials, and of the ionic migration and interfaces between the materials.
Fabricating a high-quality large-area perovskite film also remains a major challenge. Although several methods have been proposed (e.g., doctor blade, slot die, and soft cover), the production of meter-scale perovskite films using these methods is dependent on further industry-lead research [52– 55]. Researchers working in academia should aim to develop new methods and approaches to provide manufacturing companies with more potential avenues to explore.
The final issue to be addressed is that all of the certified results reported to date were obtained at locations outside of China, which makes certification a time-consuming and expensive process for Chinese researchers [56,57]. Although some institutes in China have adopted similar measurement standards, they are not yet recognized internationally. Construction of a recognized certification center in China would allow local researchers to more easily test their developments.
According to our previously reported cost analysis, the efficiency and lifetime of a meter-scale PSC module should be at least $15\%$ and 15 years, respectively, to produce an electricity generating cost lower than that of traditional energy sources [9]. Although this is a challenging target, we believe that the first commercial PSC will be produced in China through the efforts of the new talented young scientists entering the Chinese research workforce.
